The earliest occupation in the Area D sector is a multi-hut compound that signals a small settlement node emerging by the mid-8th century BC. The phase is defined by a large, partially sunken elliptical hut (H1) flanked by two smaller circular huts (H2–H3), with additional pits/trenches/depressions interspersed between them. Construction is a typical central-Italian protohistoric architecture: partially sunken foundations, wattle-and-daub walls, and a roof system with evidence consistent with thatched roofing traditions.

Chronologically, the emergence of this hut settlement is supported by radiocarbon evidence suggestive of an 8th-century start (while recognising the interpretive caution about redeposition). This phase provides the architectural baseline against which later “aggregation” and elite signalling can be measured, because the sequence will progressively formalise built space and boundaries in the following centuries.

Bibliography: Evans, J. Marilyn; Samuels, J. Troy; Motta, Laura; Naglak, Matthew; D’Acri, Mattia. 2019. “An Iron Age Settlement at Gabii: An Interim Report of the Gabii Project Excavations in Area D, 2012–2015.” Etruscan Studies 22(1–2): 6–38. https://doi.org/10.1515/etst-2018-0029;

Between the late 8th and the mid-7th century BC, the multi-hut compound that occupied the area was destroyed and rebuilt. Excavators identified traces of burning across the site, suggesting that a fire at least partly caused the destruction; the earlier huts and features were then filled with debris from the preceding occupation and the destroyed structures.

The rebuilt layout is reorganised around a single rectangular hut (H4) constructed directly above the remains of H1. Beaten-earth floors in the hut show repeated repair episodes and gradual raising of floor levels through time. The reconstructed maximum footprint is about 7 × 6 m, and internal differentiation is suggested by a rectangular stone feature interpreted as a hearth, plus postholes reused for storage/refuse (notably with thousands of barley seeds). Ceramic evidence places the construction/occupation/abandonment of H4 roughly between 730 and 630 BC, not extending beyond the mid-7th century.

Bibliography: Evans, J. Marilyn; Samuels, J. Troy; Motta, Laura; Naglak, Matthew; D’Acri, Mattia. 2019. “An Iron Age Settlement at Gabii: An Interim Report of the Gabii Project Excavations in Area D, 2012–2015.” Etruscan Studies 22(1–2): 6–38. https://doi.org/10.1515/etst-2018-0029;

Toward the end of the 7th century BC, the rectangular hut (H4) which occupied the area was destroyed (plausibly by fire) and a stone-built complex was constructed over its remains. Evans et al. identify two main monumental phases; Phase 1 includes the construction of a precinct wall, a rectangular room (R2), and a stone-lined fire pit/hearth in the late 7th or early 6th century BC.

Subsequent excavation clarified that the precinct wall likely enclosed a larger area than initially recognised, suggesting a deliberately bounded compound rather than a small “house plot”. Much of the enclosed space appears to have been open-air at this stage, with a productive zone north of R2: the in-situ hearth/fire pit and associated layers rich in burning traces, charcoal/ash, and burned ceramics/bone are interpreted as food-related production, with explicit comparison to cook-shed evidence elsewhere in Latium.

Bibliography: Evans, J. Marilyn; Samuels, J. Troy; Motta, Laura; Naglak, Matthew; D’Acri, Mattia. 2019. “An Iron Age Settlement at Gabii: An Interim Report of the Gabii Project Excavations in Area D, 2012–2015.” Etruscan Studies 22(1–2): 6–38. https://doi.org/10.1515/etst-2018-0029;

In the mid- to late 6th century BC the complex underwent a major reorganisation. The most visible change is the monumentalisation of the northern half of the compound, with the addition of a northern rectangular room (R1), constructed over an earlier productive facility), and new targeted repairs in the southern area (new stonework in the NE corner of R2; a new masonry course for the hearth/fire pit). Also, between R1 and R2, a semicircular stone-built feature was added, whose function remains uncertain (comparisons range from altar-like structures to hearths, but without decisive diagnostic traces).By the end of the 6th century BC the complex was abandoned or destroyed, and the area was subsequently repurposed as a burial ground in the late 6th–5th centuries BC. 

Later work in Area C recovered additional elements consistent with this broader compound (irregular drystone wall foundations and large beaten-earth surfaces contemporary with the Area D phases), reinforcing the interpretation that Areas C and D are adjacent parts of a single larger settlement/elite complex before the later city grid was imposed.

Bibliography: Evans, J. Marilyn; Samuels, J. Troy; Motta, Laura; Naglak, Matthew; D’Acri, Mattia. 2019. “An Iron Age Settlement at Gabii: An Interim Report of the Gabii Project Excavations in Area D, 2012–2015.” Etruscan Studies 22(1–2): 6–38. https://doi.org/10.1515/etst-2018-0029;

The “South Well” is one of the two wells associated with Temple A, built in tuff blocks around 450–440 BC near the temple’s front corners. It was cut down into grey Pliocene clay, with a concave (‘basin-shaped’) bottom at roughly 4.25 m depth. Excavation recorded 10 successive cuts, grouped into three closure-related strata: 
(I) a thin upper layer (cut 1) linked to the terrace deposits in front of the temple; 
(II) cuts 2–4 interpreted as a “cover” sealing the deeper fill and comparable to the 5th-century pavement deposits;
(III) cuts 5–10, the thickest portion (c. 2.30 m in the South Well), which produced the archaeobotanical macro-remains.

Bibliography: Coccolini & Follieri 1980

Fills included stratified soil layers used to level, remodel, or deliberately seal earlier garden layouts. Archaeologically, these fills mark different moments of re-organization:
(1) after the intensive Tiberius–Caligula planting programme (Phase 3.2);
(2) Claudian interventions (Phase 3.3) that covered parts of the garden features with added soil;
(3) later works (including Flavian drainage activity) could involve further redeposition and levelling. 

Additional samples were also taken from fills connected with features such as drainage trenches and related structures spanning Phase 3.2 through Phases 3.3/3.4, allowing the plant record to be compared across successive episodes of reconfiguration.

Bibliography: Serlorenzi, Mirella et al. 2023. Il necessario invisibile. Impastare, miscelare, cuocere e conservare: il ruolo delle ceramiche dagli Horti Lamiani. Bollettino di Archeologia On Line XIV (2023/2); Masi, Alessia et al. 2023 (online 2024). “The first extensive study of an Imperial Roman Garden in the city of Rome: integrated archaeobotanical analyses from Horti Lamiani.” Vegetation History and Archaeobotany; Ministero della Cultura, Museo Ninfeo (Horti Lamiani) (institutional page).
